WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.390
He's the breakout star of the Marvel Cinematic
Universe, but Black Panther is hardly new

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:05.680
to superheroics.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.650
Since making his debut over 50 years ago,
he's been a part of countless comics adventures,

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:13.690
and with that much action, there are bound
to be a few odd bits that you've missed.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.279
Here's what you don't know about Black Panther.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:18.019
The first black superhero

00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:21.750
Black creators were a part of comics from
the very beginning, but on the page, things

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.010
were drastically different.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.449
There were a few attempts at making comics
directed at an African-American audience,

00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:30.650
but with the early days of superheroes, most
black characters were sidekicks rooted in

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.689
unfortunate racial stereotypes.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:37.360
In 1966, however, Stan Lee and Jack Kirby
took steps to correct that by introducing

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.370
a new character in their flagship title.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:43.871
Fantastic Four #52 introduced the world to
T'Challa — better known as Black Panther

00:00:43.871 --> 00:00:46.070
— king of the African nation of Wakanda.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.890
When he was asked about it, Kirby pointed
to a very simple reason for creating the character:

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.510
he realized that there weren't any black people
in his comics, and thought that was a problem

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:54.620
to be corrected.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.629
With that, the Panther was introduced in a
story where he invited the FF to his secretive,

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:02.839
ultra high-tech homeland, only to take them
all out in a massive battle in order to test

00:01:02.839 --> 00:01:04.800
them and find them worthy of helping them.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.760
Not a bad debut.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.450
Introducing … The Coal Tiger?

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:11.970
The version of T'Challa that hit the page
in his first appearance sported the sleek,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.610
all-black costume that he's worn, with a few
modifications, ever since.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.190
Kirby's original designs, however, not only
sported a different costume, but a different

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:20.890
name: the Coal Tiger.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.551
As for how he wound up as the Black Panther,
a lot of people erroneously attribute it to

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:27.080
a connection with the activist organization
of the same name.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.340
That’s not the case — Stan Lee was inspired
by an old pulp hero — but the accidental

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.380
association with the organization led Marvel
to briefly change the character's name to

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:39.640
"The Black Leopard" in 1972’s Fantastic
Four #119.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.070
The "Coal Tiger" name wasn't completely forgotten,
though.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:46.180
In the "MC2" continuity — a version of the
Marvel Universe set a generation after the

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.250
current stories and mostly focused on Peter
Parker's daughter, Spider-Girl — T'Challa's

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.180
son T'Chaka used it as his own superheroic
codename.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.750
The Panther fights the Klan

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:59.700
While the Panther would remain a fixture in
Fantastic Four for the rest of the '60s, and

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.360
would join the Avengers in 1968, the early
'70s saw him launched into his first solo

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:05.360
adventures.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.349
It happened in the pages of Jungle Action
#6, and over the next 18 issues, which would

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:11.519
tell one of Marvel's most lauded stories.

00:02:11.519 --> 00:02:15.760
The series reached its heights in #19, the
Panther is pitted against the Ku Klux Klan.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.109
Set in Georgia, the story focused on Monica
Lynne, a singer who became the Panther's longtime

00:02:20.109 --> 00:02:22.439
love interest, and dealt heavily with racism.

00:02:22.439 --> 00:02:26.230
It's full of striking imagery, and one issue
even ends with the Panther strapped to a burning

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:30.109
cross — before he frees himself, batters
his way through the crowd with the wood still

00:02:30.109 --> 00:02:34.359
tied to his back, and returns to stomp a mudhole
in the entire group.

00:02:34.359 --> 00:02:35.579
Good vibranium

00:02:35.579 --> 00:02:39.280
Comic books are full of special metals, but
none are quite as interesting as Vibranium.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.049
In the Marvel Universe, it’s only found
in two places: Wakanda, and the dinosaur-filled

00:02:44.049 --> 00:02:46.390
Antarctic jungle known as the Savage Land.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:50.650
Wakanda's variety is known as "True Vibranium,"
and its rarity is attributed to the fact that

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.150
it's not actually native to Earth.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:55.719
Instead, the mountain from which it's mined
is actually a massive meteorite.

00:02:55.719 --> 00:02:59.650
It has the ability to store and discharge
vibrations, including the kind that come with

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:03.650
a kinetic impact, making it able to withstand
and even reflect energy.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.420
In addition to being used as part of Captain
America’s shield and even a few of Hawkeye's

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.590
arrows, you can find it in the Panther's strength-enhancing,
bulletproof costume.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.040
The Dora Milaje

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.222
When Christopher Priest and J.G.

00:03:14.222 --> 00:03:18.420
Jones relaunched Black Panther in 1998, they
delved into Wakanda and its culture in a way

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.170
that hadn't really been done before.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.650
One of the most important additions was the
idea of the Dora Milaje.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:28.950
Translated as "Adored Ones," the Dora Milaje
are highly trained, highly effective warrior

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.870
women who serve as the Black Panther's bodyguards
— and the fact that they're protecting a

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:36.950
bulletproof, super-strong member of the Avengers
should give you a good idea of just how tough

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:37.950
they are.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.580
They have a second function, though, hinted
at by their names: they're also potential

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:42.580
wives.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.140
Each member of the Dora Milaje comes from
a different faction, and as long as the Panther

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.629
doesn't favor one over any of the others,
peace is maintained.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:53.520
Which might be why, when he actually did get
married, it was to Storm of the X-Men rather

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.160
than one of them.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.469
Everett Ross sold his soul for pants

00:03:57.469 --> 00:04:00.750
If you've been paying attention to the Marvel
Cinematic Universe, then you may already be

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:05.249
familiar with Everett K. Ross, the CIA agent
assigned to deal with some of the more official

00:04:05.249 --> 00:04:07.920
aspects of life in a universe full of superheroes.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.870
In the movies, he's played by Martin Freeman,
and made his first appearance in Captain America:

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:12.870
Civil War.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.090
In the comics, however, he had a bit of a
rocky start.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:19.390
As an agent working for the State Department,
he was assigned to be a liason to Black Panther

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:21.070
during the hero's visit to America.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:25.420
Needless to say, the self-proclaimed "king
of the useless white boys" was woefully unprepared

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.790
to be pulled into the world of superheroics.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:32.140
After losing his pants in a mud-wrestling
match, Ross found himself visited by Mephisto,

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:34.730
the closest thing that the Marvel Universe
has to the devil.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:38.560
Sitting next to each other in an apartment,
with Ross on the couch and Mephisto on a flaming

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.160
throne of skulls that he conjured up from
the depths of Hell, the two characters shared

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.040
a moment when Mephisto granted Ross a new
pair of jeans, leaving Ross wondering if he

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.570
had accidentally sold his soul for pants.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:52.120
The Black Panther movie that wasn't

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.530
Superhero fans are undoubtedly familiar with
Wesley Snipes from his role as Blade in the

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.170
movie series of the same name.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:01.770
Hitting screens in 1998, the movie not only
gave Marvel its first cinematic hit, it also

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.920
showed that second- and third-tier characters
could be given a new life in film with the

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.280
right idea behind them.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.450
But what even diehard fans might not know
is that Blade wasn't Snipes' first attempt

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:13.170
to bring a superhero to life.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:17.220
Long before Ryan Coogler took the helm with
Chadwick Boseman, Snipes was dead set on playing

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.060
the lead in a Black Panther movie.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.890
In the mid-’90s, Snipes was in talks for
a film that would use the "fantastic, glorious

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:27.360
periods of African empires and African royalty"
as the basis to bring an inspirational new

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.850
superhero to the screen.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:33.540
He wanted Wakanda, Vibranium, the force field,
and the kind of comics-accurate costume that

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.730
would require him to be, and we quote, "just
straight bodied up."

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:40.150
Unfortunately, as we all know, there's always
someone ice skating uphill.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.580
While Snipes met with director John Singleton
and screenwriter Terry Hayes, the project

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:44.790
never came together.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:49.280
Instead, Snipes went on to take a very successful
risk as the Daywalker, and says today that

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.670
he supports the new film "1,000 percent."

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:52.690
Thanks for watching!

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.040
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.370
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

